---
.title = "iroh: encrypted peer-to-peer",
.date = @date("2026-06-17T00:00:00"),
.author = "Jim Isaacs",
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

iroh is pancake's encrypted transport, and the one that lets two peers reach each
other without a server in the middle. A session's bytes ride
[QUIC](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9000.html) carried by
[iroh](https://www.iroh.computer/), and a peer is named by a self-authenticating
key rather than a `host:port`. Everything above the wire stays the same OCapN
pancake speaks over TCP, so switching to iroh changes only two things: how a peer
is addressed, and that its channel is now encrypted and authenticated end to end.

## One key does three jobs

Each endpoint is an Ed25519 keypair, and pancake makes that single key carry three
roles at once:

- it is the **iroh node id** the transport authenticates the QUIC channel against;
- it is the **OCapN locator designator**, the name you dial in an
  `ocapn://<node-id>.iroh` URI;
- it is the **session signing key** that signs the `op:start-session` handshake.

[One keypair fills all three roles, so the peer you dialed is provably the peer that signed the session.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-iroh-key.svg'))

`zig build iroh-interop` checks that knot on a live wire from both ends: the
session public key the partner proved, the node id the transport authenticated, and
the designator in the signed locator are all the same key. There is no separate
certificate to trust and no address to spoof, because the address *is* the key.

The identity also outlives the process that minted it. An endpoint bound from the
same seed comes back with the same node id at a fresh network address. A
sturdyref (a node id plus a swiss number) therefore still names the same capability
after a restart. The interop check step proves that too, by tearing an endpoint down and
reaching the same published cap through the locator recorded before the restart.

## Reaching a peer

A peer hands you its node id; how that id turns into a route is a *reachability
posture* you choose when you bind the endpoint. pancake exposes four:

| Posture | How a dial resolves | Proven by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `direct` | the dialer carries the peer's direct socket addresses as locator hints | `zig build iroh-interop` |
| `local_discovery` | an in-process address book resolves the id, no relay, fully deterministic | `zig build iroh-interop` |
| `discovery` | the public n0 relays plus DNS/pkarr resolve the id over the open internet | `zig build iroh-reachability` |
| `discovery_no_relay` | DNS/pkarr resolve the id, but with relay fallback disabled: published direct addresses only | posture governance only: `zig build bindings-iroh-redeem-posture` |

`direct` and `local_discovery` are deterministic and run as the standard interop
check step. `discovery` reaches real n0 infrastructure: a server publishes its node info
to pkarr on bind, and a client dials by the 64-hex id with no hints. A session that
opens therefore proves the route was discovered, not hinted. Because it depends on
public infrastructure and propagation timing, it is opt-in and never part of the
in-tree `zig build check`. `discovery_no_relay` is the same path with relay
fallback disabled. No check step dials it live (that would need a publicly addressable
host), so its cited check step proves something narrower and self-contained: a hint-less give is
governed by whatever posture the endpoint configured, this one included.

## A separate artifact, by design

iroh's QUIC stack is Rust, so it rides a sidecar staticlib that pancake keeps in
its own compiled artifact, `libpancake_net_transport_iroh`
([ADR-0055](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0055-iroh-netlayer-sidecar.md)).
The base archives stay pure Zig and dependency-free; a program links iroh only when
it wants it. `zig build iroh-abi` builds that companion archive and its header
(it needs `cargo`).

The same split shows up in the bindings. Each one keeps iroh in a per-transport
module: the `@pancake/iroh` package, `pancake.iroh`, `pancake-iroh.rkt`, and Rust's
opt-in `iroh` feature. `connect` loads that module lazily the first time it sees an
`iroh` locator (Rust compiles it in behind the feature instead), so a program that
only ever speaks TCP never loads it. Hosting over iroh adds one verb, a
listen-over-iroh call each binding spells its own way (`listenIroh` in TypeScript,
`listen_iroh` in Python). Dialing a peer over iroh goes through the same `connect`
as TCP; the only difference a caller sees is the address.

## Scale: many connections, one thread

One connection per OS thread is what `iroh-interop` proves. For fan-out, a loop
host drives every connection from one readiness descriptor
([ADR-0057](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0057-iroh-completion-loop-bridge.md)):
`zig build iroh-loop` and `bindings-iroh-loop`. Round-trip cost vs cleartext TCP:
[Performance]($link.page('performance')). Sidecar heap is separate from the
checked ~2 KiB sans-io core.

## Three-party handoff over iroh

Same CapTP introduction as on TCP
([Concepts]($link.page('concepts'))): the recipient dials the exporter by node id
on a fresh encrypted session. Proof: `zig build iroh-mesh`.

## See it run

Every claim here is a runnable check step (each needs `cargo`, and the live discovery one
needs the public internet):

```sh
zig build iroh-interop       # keyed conversation, dial-by-id, key unification, durable identity
zig build iroh-reachability  # dial-by-id over n0 relays + DNS/pkarr (opt-in, public internet)
zig build iroh-loop          # many concurrent connections served from one readiness loop
zig build iroh-mesh          # three-party handoff reached over iroh by node id
zig build iroh-abi           # build libpancake_net_transport_iroh and its header
```

For a full application over the encrypted channel, the [rooms]($link.page('examples'))
chat runs across five languages over iroh with `zig build demo-rooms-iroh`. The
[Transports]($link.page('transports')) page sets iroh beside the TCP and in-process
residents, and [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) explains locators, hints, and
sturdyrefs in general.
